# JAVA CORE / CONCURRENCY
# ======================================================================

def topic_java_concurrency_jmm():
    return itopic(
        "Java Concurrency & the Java Memory Model", ESSENTIAL, STUDY,
        short_overview="How multiple threads safely share mutable state on the JVM, and the memory-visibility rules (JMM) that make 'safely' precise rather than folklore.",
        why="Modern servers (including this project's own Spring Boot Customer app under Tomcat's thread-per-request model) run many requests concurrently on multiple CPU cores. Without an explicit, correct concurrency model, two threads touching the same object produce results that depend on timing — a bug that passes every single-threaded test and fails unpredictably in production under load. Concurrency is not optional at senior level: it's the difference between a service that degrades linearly under load and one that corrupts data or deadlocks under load.",
        history_evolution="Java shipped with `synchronized` and `Thread` from Java 1.0 (1996) — coarse, error-prone locking. The original Java Memory Model (pre-2004) was informally specified and had exploitable holes (double-checked locking was famously broken under it). JSR-133 (led by Doug Lea, Bill Pugh, and Jeremy Manson, finalized in Java 5, 2004) formally rewrote the JMM, fixed `volatile` semantics, and introduced `java.util.concurrent` (`ExecutorService`, `ConcurrentHashMap`, `CountDownLatch`, atomics) — Doug Lea's `util.concurrent` library predated this and was folded in almost verbatim. Java 8 (2014) added `CompletableFuture` and lambdas, making async composition tractable. Java 21 (2023, LTS) shipped Virtual Threads (Project Loom, led by Ron Pressler), the largest concurrency shift since JSR-133 — decoupling 'a unit of concurrency' from 'an OS thread' for the first time.",
        why_now="Needed whenever a service must serve more than one request at a time and any state (an in-memory cache, a counter, a shared connection pool, a singleton service bean) is touched by more than one thread. Not needed for a single-threaded batch script with no shared mutable state. Overengineering to hand-roll locks when a higher-level `java.util.concurrent` primitive already solves the problem correctly.",
        big_picture="Business requirement: handle many simultaneous users without one user's request corrupting another's data or waiting unnecessarily. Functional requirement: correctness under concurrent access. NFRs: throughput (requests/sec), latency under load, no data races. Architecture boundary: which objects are shared across requests (Spring beans are singletons by default — a stateless `@Service` is safe, a `@Service` with a mutable instance field is a live concurrency bug) versus which are request-scoped (method-local variables, one per thread, inherently safe). Data flow: each HTTP request gets its own thread (or, with virtual threads, its own lightweight continuation); shared state is only the exception, not the default.",
        design_intellect="The core idea protected by the JMM is a *happens-before* ordering: without an explicit synchronization action (a lock, a `volatile` read/write, a thread start/join), the JVM and CPU are both free to reorder and cache operations for performance, so one thread has no guarantee it will ever observe another thread's write. The invariant `synchronized`/`volatile`/atomics protect is: 'if A happens-before B, every write A made is visible to B.' This is chosen over naive 'just don't reorder anything' because unconstrained reordering is what makes single-threaded code fast (CPU pipelining, compiler optimization, per-core caching) — the JMM's job is to give you a narrow, correct escape hatch for the multithreaded case without paying that cost everywhere.",
        how_exactly="Business request arrives -> browser opens TCP connection -> kernel accepts and hands the socket to Tomcat's acceptor -> Tomcat's thread pool assigns a worker thread (or, on Java 21+ with virtual threads enabled, a virtual thread mounted on a small carrier-thread pool) -> that thread runs Spring's DispatcherServlet -> Controller -> Service -> Repository, entirely on its own call stack -> if the Service touches a shared object (a singleton bean's field, a static cache), the JVM's memory model governs whether this thread's write/read is visible to a concurrently-running thread on another core -> a lock (`synchronized`, `ReentrantLock`) forces mutual exclusion plus a happens-before edge at acquire/release -> an atomic (`AtomicInteger`) uses a CPU compare-and-swap instruction instead of a lock -> the response is built and returned down the same call stack, the thread returns to the pool.",
        low_level_internals="Each CPU core has its own L1/L2 cache; a write from one core isn't automatically visible to another core's cache without a cache-coherence protocol message (MESI) — `volatile` in Java compiles to bytecode that forces the JIT to emit the necessary memory barriers (store/load fences) so the CPU cannot silently keep a stale cached copy. Every Java thread maps to a real OS thread (pre-Loom) with its own JVM stack (frames, local variables) but shares the JVM heap (objects). `synchronized` uses the object's header (a mark word) to implement a monitor — uncontended lock/unlock is a cheap CAS on that word (biased/thin locking); a contended lock parks the OS thread (a real context switch, ~1-10 microseconds, plus scheduling latency) via the OS's futex (Linux) or equivalent. `AtomicInteger.incrementAndGet()` compiles to a `lock cmpxchg`/`lock xadd` x86 instruction — no OS involvement, just a locked memory-bus operation, which is why atomics are far cheaper than locks under mild contention.",
        who="Tomcat's HTTP thread pool (or virtual-thread scheduler on Java 21+), the JVM's JIT compiler (memory-barrier insertion), the CPU's cache-coherence hardware (MESI protocol), the OS scheduler (context switching parked threads), and the Spring container (deciding bean scope, which decides what's actually shared).",
        industry="Nearly every backend job posting above mid-level lists 'concurrency' or 'multithreading' explicitly; it's one of the most common Java interview filter questions because it reliably separates candidates who've internalized the model from those who've memorized `synchronized` as a keyword.",
        business="A single un-synchronized shared counter (e.g. a naive in-memory rate limiter or inventory count) can silently under- or over-count under real concurrent load, translating directly into overselling inventory or letting a rate limit be bypassed — a correctness bug with direct revenue/trust impact, not just a crash.",
        product="Concurrency correctness is invisible when the product works and catastrophic when it doesn't (data corruption, phantom double-charges) — it's a non-negotiable quality bar a PM will never explicitly request because they assume it as a baseline.",
        cost_economics="Coarse locking (one big lock around everything) is cheap to write but caps throughput — every request serializes behind the lock regardless of core count, wasting the very hardware you're paying for. Fine-grained locking or lock-free (atomic) structures cost more engineering time up front but let throughput actually scale with CPU cores, which is usually the cheaper trade at any real production scale (renting more cores is expensive; hand-rolled fine-grained concurrency correctness is expensive once; getting it wrong is far more expensive — an incident).",
        failure_incident="Symptom: an in-memory counter or cache occasionally returns a stale or wrong value only under real production load, never in local single-threaded testing. Telemetry: no exception thrown (the classic tell — visibility bugs corrupt data silently). Hypothesis: a shared mutable field is read/written from multiple request threads without synchronization. Diagnosis: code review finds a plain (non-volatile, non-atomic, non-locked) field on a singleton `@Service` bean being mutated per-request. Root cause: missing happens-before edge — one thread's write was never guaranteed visible to another. Fix: replace with `AtomicLong`/`AtomicReference`, or make the field genuinely request-scoped, or add correct locking. Regression: a concurrency stress test (many threads hammering the endpoint) added to the suite so it fails fast next time, since a single-threaded test can never catch this class of bug. Business impact: silently wrong counts (inventory, rate limits, sequence numbers) reaching customers or downstream systems.",
        alternatives_tradeoffs="`synchronized`/locks (simple, but block and can deadlock; cost scales with contention) vs. atomics (`java.util.concurrent.atomic`, lock-free, fast under contention, but only for single-variable updates) vs. immutable objects (no shared mutable state at all — the simplest correct answer whenever the data doesn't need to change in place) vs. `ConcurrentHashMap`/other concurrent collections (purpose-built, better than hand-rolled locking around a plain `HashMap`) vs. virtual threads + structured concurrency (Java 21+, changes the *scale* of concurrency, not the visibility rules — you still need the JMM underneath).",
        scenario="STUDY_SCENARIO: an in-memory 'requests remaining today' rate limiter implemented as a plain `int` field on a singleton bean works fine in a demo, then under real concurrent load starts occasionally allowing one extra request past the limit because two threads read-then-increment the same stale value (a classic check-then-act race). Rehearsing this scenario end-to-end (symptom -> diagnosis -> fix with `AtomicInteger` or a proper distributed counter -> regression test) is a strong System Design + coding interview answer even without having hit this exact bug in production.",
        interview_mode=im(
            sec_15="Concurrency is about correctly sharing mutable state across threads; the Java Memory Model defines exactly when one thread's writes are guaranteed visible to another, via happens-before relationships established by locks, volatile, and thread start/join.",
            sec_30="Without synchronization, both the compiler/JIT and CPU are free to reorder and cache reads/writes for performance, so one thread has no guarantee it ever sees another thread's write. `synchronized`, `volatile`, and `java.util.concurrent` atomics each establish a happens-before edge, at different costs — full mutual exclusion, a memory-barrier-only guarantee, or a lock-free CPU instruction, respectively.",
            min_2="Explain JMM history (JSR-133 fixing double-checked locking), the happens-before model, then walk one concrete example: an unsynchronized counter on a singleton Spring bean corrupted under load, diagnosed via a symptom with no exception, fixed with AtomicInteger, and locked in with a concurrency stress test.",
            deep_technical="Ask them to explain why plain `int` isn't safe even for a single increment (read-modify-write isn't atomic), what a memory barrier actually does at the CPU level (store/load fences enforcing cache-coherence ordering), and the cost difference between `synchronized` (potential OS-level park/unpark) and an atomic CAS instruction.",
            system_design="Frame as: every stateless service in a horizontally-scaled architecture avoids this whole class of problem by design — the real System Design answer to 'how do you handle concurrency at scale' is usually 'push shared mutable state out of the application into a datastore built for it (DB row locks, Redis atomic ops), and keep application instances themselves stateless.'",
            business_value="A silent concurrency bug in a counter or limiter directly costs money (oversold inventory, bypassed rate limits) without ever throwing an exception — framing this as a business-risk question, not just a technical one, shows senior-level judgment.",
            incident_debugging="Walk the failure_incident section verbatim: no exception, works locally, fails only under real concurrent load — the signature of a visibility bug, not a logic bug.",
            followups=[
                {"q": "Why isn't `i++` atomic?", "a": "It's actually three operations (read, add, write) on the underlying bytecode/CPU level; two threads can interleave between them and lose an update."},
                {"q": "When would you choose `synchronized` over an atomic?", "a": "When you need to update more than one variable together as one indivisible unit — atomics only guarantee atomicity for a single variable."},
            ],
        ),
        current_status_2026="Virtual threads (finalized Java 21, 2023) are now the default recommendation for high-concurrency I/O-bound services on modern Spring Boot (3.2+) — `spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true` — because they let a thread-per-request programming model scale to hundreds of thousands of concurrent requests without the old per-OS-thread memory cost, while the underlying JMM happens-before rules are completely unchanged. Structured concurrency (JEP 480, still previewing as of late-2025/2026) is the next planned refinement, aimed at making related concurrent tasks fail and cancel together rather than leaking.",
    )


def topic_jvm_memory_gc():
    return itopic(
        "JVM Memory Model & Garbage Collection", ESSENTIAL, STUDY,
        short_overview="How the JVM allocates, organizes, and reclaims memory (heap, stack, metaspace) automatically, and what that costs in latency and tuning effort.",
        why="Every object a Java program creates lives somewhere in memory that must eventually be reclaimed; automatic garbage collection is the feature that makes Java (and the JVM ecosystem generally) productive compared to manual memory management, but it isn't free — GC pauses, memory leaks despite GC, and OutOfMemoryErrors are among the most common real production incidents in any JVM-based backend, including a Spring Boot service like this project's Customer app.",
        history_evolution="Java's original GC (1996) was a simple, fully-stopping mark-sweep collector. The generational hypothesis (most objects die young) drove the serial/parthroughput collectors of the early 2000s. CMS (Concurrent Mark Sweep, Java 1.4+) reduced pause times by doing most work concurrently with the application but suffered from heap fragmentation and was deprecated in Java 9, removed in Java 14. G1 (Garbage-First, designed at Sun/Oracle, production-default since Java 9, 2017) reorganized the heap into many small regions so it could collect the most garbage-dense regions first and predict pause times. ZGC (Java 15+ production) and Shenandoah (Red Hat, OpenJDK) pushed pause times to sub-millisecond even on multi-terabyte heaps by doing almost all work concurrently, using colored pointers/load barriers.",
        why_now="Matters the moment a JVM service runs long enough and allocates enough to actually trigger collections under real load — which is essentially always for a running server (as opposed to a short-lived CLI tool). Overkill to hand-tune GC flags for a low-traffic service well within default heap limits; premature GC tuning without a measured problem is a classic time-waste.",
        big_picture="Business requirement: predictable response latency and no crashes from running out of memory. NFR: p99 latency must not spike from GC pauses; availability must not be interrupted by OOM crashes. Architecture: heap size is a deployment/infrastructure decision (container memory limit -> `-Xmx`), not just a code concern — under-provisioning either crashes the process (OOM-killed by the OS/container orchestrator) or forces constant, disruptive collection.",
        design_intellect="The core idea is the generational hypothesis: most objects are short-lived (a request-scoped DTO, a temporary string), so segregating memory into a small, fast-to-collect Young generation (using cheap copying collection) and a larger, rarely-collected Old generation (using more expensive mark-compact) dramatically reduces average collection cost versus treating the whole heap uniformly. The invariant protected is: an object is never reclaimed while still reachable from a GC root (a thread stack, a static field) — reachability, not reference-counting, is what Java GC tracks, which correctly handles cycles that naive reference counting cannot.",
        how_exactly="`new Customer(...)` in a Controller/Service method -> JVM allocates the object in Eden space (Young generation) via a fast bump-pointer allocation -> if the object survives a Young GC (a minor GC, stop-the-world but brief, copying live objects to a Survivor space) enough times, it's promoted to the Old generation -> when Old generation fills, a major/full GC runs (with G1, this is normally incremental and mostly concurrent) -> unreachable objects (no live GC-root path reaches them) are identified via mark-and-sweep/compact and their memory reclaimed -> the request's response is built and returned regardless of when this happens, since GC runs on its own background/pause cycle independent of any single request's logic.",
        low_level_internals="Heap memory is a large, JVM-managed region of the process's virtual address space; Metaspace (off-heap, replacing PermGen since Java 8) holds class metadata; each thread has its own small JVM stack (method frames, local variables, primitive values) separate from the heap. A minor GC is a stop-the-world pause (all application threads suspended at a safepoint) while live Young-gen objects are copied — pause time scales with live-object count, not heap size, which is why G1's region-based approach can bound pause time predictably. `-Xmx`/`-Xms` set max/initial heap; `-XX:+UseG1GC` (default since Java 9) selects the collector; a container's cgroup memory limit, if the JVM doesn't correctly detect it (fixed by Java 10's container-awareness), can cause the OS OOM-killer to kill the whole process even though the JVM thinks it has headroom.",
        who="The JVM's garbage collector threads (G1's background marking threads), the JIT compiler (allocation fast-path inlining), the OS/container's memory cgroup and OOM-killer, and the operator who sets `-Xmx`/container memory limits at deployment time.",
        industry="GC tuning and 'explain generational GC' are staple senior Java interview topics precisely because they distinguish engineers who understand what's happening under a `new` keyword from those who treat the JVM as a black box; they also directly gate real cloud cost (heap size -> container memory -> $/month).",
        business="An OOM crash is total downtime for every user hitting that instance until it restarts — a memory leak that grows slowly can degrade a service for days before anyone notices, then fail catastrophically at the worst possible moment (peak traffic).",
        product="Users experience GC pauses as intermittent slow requests with no obvious code-level cause — a product team reporting 'the app is randomly slow sometimes' is very often, on investigation, a GC or connection-pool-starvation symptom.",
        cost_economics="Heap size is a direct cloud cost lever (bigger heap needs a bigger container instance type); undersized heap causes more frequent GC (CPU cost, latency cost) while oversized heap wastes provisioned memory that's paid for whether used or not — right-sizing from measured live-object volume, not guessing, is the economically correct approach.",
        failure_incident="Symptom: response latency has periodic spikes (visible as a sawtooth pattern in a latency graph) or the process eventually OOM-crashes after running for hours/days. Telemetry: GC logs (`-Xlog:gc`) or a heap-usage metric climbing steadily and never fully recovering after collections (the signature of a leak, as opposed to normal sawtooth Young-gen behavior). Hypothesis: either GC pause time under real load, or objects being retained (a growing cache with no eviction, a listener never unregistered, a static collection appended to and never cleared). Diagnosis: a heap dump (`jmap`/container-triggered on OOM) analyzed for the dominant retained-object type and its GC-root path. Root cause example: an in-memory cache with no eviction policy growing unbounded. Fix: bound the cache (size/TTL) or move it to an external store. Regression: a load/soak test that runs long enough to reveal a slow leak, since a short test never will. Business impact: intermittent slow responses degrade user trust; an eventual crash is full downtime.",
        alternatives_tradeoffs="G1 (default, balanced throughput/pause-time, good for most services including this project's) vs. ZGC/Shenandoah (near-zero pause even on huge heaps, at some throughput/CPU cost, justified only at large heap sizes or very strict latency SLOs) vs. Serial GC (single-threaded, fine only for small heaps/short-lived tools) vs. avoiding the question entirely by minimizing allocation in hot paths (fewer objects created means less GC work regardless of collector choice).",
        scenario="STUDY_SCENARIO: a Spring Boot service adds an in-memory `Map` used as a cache for expensive lookups, ships without an eviction policy, runs fine in staging (short-lived, low traffic) and then OOM-crashes in production after several days once the map has grown to contain every distinct key ever seen. Rehearsing the full symptom->heap-dump->root-cause->bounded-cache fix->soak-test-regression trace is directly transferable interview material even without having personally debugged this exact incident.",
        interview_mode=im(
            sec_15="The JVM automatically allocates objects on the heap and reclaims unreachable ones via garbage collection, using a generational strategy (short-lived objects collected cheaply and often in a Young generation, long-lived ones rarely in an Old generation).",
            sec_30="Most objects die young, so the JVM segregates the heap into Young (cheap, frequent, stop-the-world-but-brief copying collection) and Old (rarer, more expensive) generations. Modern default collector G1 further splits the heap into regions so it can prioritize collecting the most garbage-dense regions first and predict pause times, rather than pausing the whole heap uniformly.",
            min_2="Cover the history (serial -> CMS -> G1 -> ZGC), the generational hypothesis and why it works, then walk a concrete leak scenario: unbounded in-memory cache, slow heap growth invisible until an OOM crash days later, diagnosed via a heap dump's dominator tree, fixed by bounding the cache.",
            deep_technical="Explain reachability (GC roots: thread stacks, static fields) versus reference counting, and why reachability correctly handles cycles that reference counting can't; explain what a stop-the-world safepoint actually is.",
            system_design="Heap/container sizing is a capacity-planning decision: undersized heap trades cloud cost savings for GC-induced latency and OOM risk; this is a concrete, quantifiable trade-off to walk through in a System Design interview's cost-and-scale section.",
            business_value="A slow memory leak is a silent, deferred outage — connect this to the business cost of an unplanned crash at peak traffic versus the cheap cost of a soak test that would have caught it in staging.",
            incident_debugging="Use the failure_incident trace: steadily-climbing heap usage graph that never fully recovers is the specific signature that distinguishes a leak from normal GC sawtooth behavior.",
        ),
        current_status_2026="G1 remains the default collector for most Spring Boot services in 2026. ZGC's generational mode (finalized Java 21+) closed most of its former throughput gap versus G1, making it an increasingly common choice for latency-sensitive services even at moderate heap sizes, not just huge ones. Java 17 and 21 (both LTS) are the current mainstream production baselines; this project's own app runs Java 17.",
    )


