def topic_spring_request_lifecycle():
    return itopic(
        "Spring Request Lifecycle & Dependency Injection", ESSENTIAL, CURRENT,
        short_overview="What actually happens, component by component, from an HTTP request hitting Tomcat to a JSON response leaving a Spring Boot app — and how DI wires the objects involved.",
        why="Almost every senior Java backend interview probes whether a candidate actually understands what 'Spring does the wiring for you' means mechanically, rather than treating annotations as magic — this is the single most-asked 'explain what happens when a request comes in' question in Java backend interviews, and this project's own Customer app (CustomerController/CustomerService/CustomerRepository) is a real, inspectable instance of exactly this lifecycle.",
        history_evolution="The Spring Framework (Rod Johnson, 2003, growing out of his book 'Expert One-on-One J2EE Design and Development') introduced Inversion of Control / Dependency Injection as a reaction against heavyweight, hard-to-test J2EE/EJB container coupling — instead of a component looking up its dependencies from a container, the container hands them in. Spring MVC (mid-2000s) added a DispatcherServlet-based front-controller web layer. Spring Boot (2014) removed the historically enormous manual XML/Java-config burden via auto-configuration and embedded servers (Tomcat/Jetty/Undertow bundled in the JAR itself, so 'the app IS the server' rather than being deployed into one).",
        why_now="Needed for any nontrivial web application where business logic must be testable in isolation from the web/data layers — DI is what makes a Service unit-testable with a mocked Repository. Overkill for a genuinely trivial single-file script with no layering, but essentially every real backend service benefits.",
        big_picture="Business requirement: turn an incoming HTTP request into a validated business action and a correct response. Architecture: a layered design — Controller (HTTP concerns: routing, status codes, request/response shape), Service (business logic, transaction boundaries), Repository (data access) — each layer depending only on the abstraction below it, wired together by the Spring container (`ApplicationContext`) at startup rather than by manual `new` calls scattered through the code.",
        design_intellect="The core idea is inversion of control: instead of a class constructing its own dependencies (tight coupling, hard to substitute in tests), the framework constructs the dependency graph once at startup and injects finished objects into constructors. The invariant protected is testability and substitutability — a `CustomerService` that receives a `CustomerRepository` via its constructor can be tested with a fake/mock Repository with zero framework involvement, and the real Repository can be swapped (H2 today, Postgres later) without touching Service code.",
        how_exactly="Browser sends HTTP request -> DNS resolves host -> TCP handshake -> request bytes arrive at the embedded Tomcat's socket -> kernel hands the accepted connection to a Tomcat worker thread -> Tomcat parses the HTTP request and calls Spring's front controller, `DispatcherServlet` -> `DispatcherServlet` consults `HandlerMapping` to find which `@RestController` method matches the URL+verb -> Spring resolves method arguments (`@PathVariable`, `@RequestBody` deserialized from JSON via Jackson, `@Valid` triggering Bean Validation) -> the Controller method (already holding an injected `CustomerService` reference, wired at application startup) calls the Service -> the Service (holding an injected `CustomerRepository`) executes business logic and calls the Repository -> Spring Data JPA's proxy implementation translates the Repository call into a JPQL/SQL query -> Hibernate obtains a JDBC connection from the HikariCP pool -> the query executes against the database and rows come back -> Hibernate maps rows to entity objects -> the Service returns to the Controller -> the Controller's return value is serialized back to JSON by Jackson via an `HttpMessageConverter` -> `DispatcherServlet` writes the HTTP response -> Tomcat sends the response bytes back over the same TCP connection -> the thread returns to the pool.",
        low_level_internals="At startup, Spring scans the classpath (`@ComponentScan`) for annotated classes, builds a dependency graph, and instantiates beans in dependency order (a `BeanFactory`/`ApplicationContext` topologically resolving constructor dependencies), storing singleton instances in an internal map keyed by bean name/type — this is why a missing dependency shows up as a startup failure ('NoSuchBeanDefinitionException'), not a runtime NullPointerException, catching wiring errors before any request is ever served. Each HTTP request runs on its own JVM thread with its own stack frame per method call (Controller frame calls Service frame calls Repository frame); the injected beans themselves are typically singletons shared across all threads (which is exactly why Service beans must avoid mutable instance state, per Java Concurrency).",
        who="Tomcat (embedded, HTTP transport), Spring's `ApplicationContext`/`BeanFactory` (object graph construction), Jackson (JSON <-> Java object marshalling), Hibernate (JPA implementation, SQL generation), HikariCP (connection pooling), the underlying database, and the JVM thread executing the whole call stack.",
        industry="'Walk me through what happens when a request hits your Spring Boot app' is one of the single most common senior Java interview questions across the industry because it reveals depth of understanding versus surface familiarity with annotations.",
        business="A correctly layered request lifecycle is what lets a team change the database (H2 -> Postgres), add validation, or add caching without touching unrelated code — directly reducing the cost and risk of every future change.",
        product="Response time and correctness the product/user actually experiences are the sum of every step in this trace; a slow step anywhere (a missing index at the Repository layer, an oversized JSON payload at serialization) is invisible to product management but fully explains a 'the app feels slow' complaint.",
        cost_economics="Each layer boundary (Controller/Service/Repository) costs a small amount of indirection (extra method calls, extra objects) in exchange for testability and replaceability — negligible CPU cost versus the engineering-time savings of being able to test and change layers independently; the real cost driver in this whole trace is almost always the database round trip, not the in-process Spring machinery.",
        failure_incident="Symptom: `NoSuchBeanDefinitionException` or a circular-dependency startup failure. Telemetry: application fails to start, stack trace names the missing/circular bean. Diagnosis: a Service depends on a Repository/other Service that either isn't annotated as a Spring-managed component or is part of a dependency cycle (A needs B, B needs A). Root cause: incorrect or missing component scanning/annotation, or a genuine circular-dependency design flaw. Fix: correct the annotation, or (for real cycles) break the cycle by extracting shared logic into a third bean. Regression: this class of error is caught at startup for every future change — the true 'test' is the application successfully starting, which is why Spring context-load tests exist. Business impact: none in production if caught pre-deploy (as it always is, since startup fails immediately) — the real risk is if it's caught late in a CI/CD pipeline right before a release deadline.",
        alternatives_tradeoffs="Constructor injection (this project's convention; makes dependencies explicit and immutable, fails fast at startup if missing) vs. field injection (`@Autowired` on a field directly — more concise but hides dependencies, makes unit testing without a Spring context harder, widely considered worse practice) vs. no DI framework at all (manual object wiring — simple for a tiny app, unmanageable at real scale where dozens of services depend on each other).",
        scenario=None,
        interview_mode=im(
            sec_15="A request flows Controller (HTTP concerns) -> Service (business logic) -> Repository (data access), with Spring's container having already wired each layer's dependencies together at startup via constructor injection.",
            sec_30="DispatcherServlet routes the request to the right Controller method, Spring deserializes and validates the request body, the Controller calls an already-injected Service, the Service calls an already-injected Repository, Spring Data JPA/Hibernate turns that into SQL via a pooled JDBC connection, and the result is serialized back to JSON on the way out — the whole dependency graph was built once at startup, not per-request.",
            min_2="Walk the full how_exactly trace end to end using this project's own CustomerController/CustomerService/CustomerRepository as the concrete example, then explain why constructor injection over field injection (explicit, immutable, fails fast at startup).",
            deep_technical="Explain how Spring resolves the dependency graph at startup (classpath scanning, topological bean instantiation) and why a missing bean fails at startup rather than at first use.",
            system_design="Use the layering itself as the System Design answer to 'how would you structure this service so the database can be swapped later' — because Service depends only on the Repository interface, not its implementation.",
            business_value="Testability from DI is what lets a team ship changes fast without a full manual regression pass on every layer — directly reduces delivery cost and risk.",
        ),
        current_status_2026="Spring Boot 3.x (current in 2026) requires Java 17+ and Jakarta EE namespaces (`jakarta.*` replacing `javax.*` since the Boot 3.0/Spring Framework 6 migration in 2022); this project's own app is on Spring Boot 4.1.1. Virtual-thread support (`spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true`) is the most significant recent evolution to this exact request lifecycle, changing what a 'worker thread' handling a request actually costs at scale without changing the logical flow described above.",
    )


def topic_spring_transactions_aop():
    return itopic(
        "Spring Transactions & AOP Proxies", ESSENTIAL, STUDY,
        short_overview="How @Transactional actually works under the hood via dynamic proxies, and the concrete ways that mechanism silently fails to apply.",
        why="`@Transactional` is one of the most-used and most-misunderstood annotations in Spring — a huge fraction of 'why didn't my transaction roll back' production bugs and interview questions trace to not understanding that it's implemented as a proxy wrapping the real method call, which has sharp, specific edge cases.",
        history_evolution="Spring's AOP (Aspect-Oriented Programming) module (early 2000s) let cross-cutting concerns (transactions, security, logging) be applied declaratively instead of hand-written in every method. `@Transactional` (building on this) became Spring's primary transaction-management API, wrapping the older, more verbose programmatic `PlatformTransactionManager` API. Spring's use of dynamic proxies (JDK interface proxies, or CGLIB subclass proxies for concrete classes) predates and directly informed how the framework later implemented other cross-cutting concerns (caching's `@Cacheable`, security's method-level `@PreAuthorize` all use the same proxy mechanism).",
        why_now="Needed whenever a single business operation must perform multiple database writes that must all succeed or all roll back together (e.g. debit one account and credit another). Unnecessary overhead/complexity for a single, naturally-atomic single-row write, where the database's own per-statement atomicity is already sufficient.",
        big_picture="Business requirement: a multi-step business operation must never leave the database in a partially-applied state if any step fails partway through. Architecture: `@Transactional` is typically placed on Service-layer methods (the natural unit-of-work boundary — one business operation), never on Repository methods (too fine-grained) or Controllers (too coarse, would hold a transaction open across serialization/network concerns).",
        design_intellect="The core idea is a dynamic proxy: Spring never modifies your `@Transactional` method's bytecode directly — instead, the bean actually injected everywhere is a proxy object that wraps your real object, starts a transaction before delegating to your real method, and commits/rolls back after it returns (commit on normal return, rollback by default only on an unchecked exception). The invariant protected is 'the annotation only works through the proxy' — which is exactly why a self-invocation (a method inside the same class calling another `@Transactional` method directly, `this.otherMethod()`) bypasses the proxy entirely and the transaction silently does not apply.",
        how_exactly="A Controller calls `customerService.transferBalance(...)` -> because `customerService` is actually a CGLIB subclass proxy (not the raw `CustomerServiceImpl`), the proxy's method intercepts the call first -> the proxy asks Spring's `PlatformTransactionManager` to begin a transaction, which asks the JDBC `Connection` (borrowed from the HikariCP pool for the duration) to turn off auto-commit and start a DB transaction -> the proxy then delegates to the real method body -> the real method performs its repository calls, all using that same bound connection (Spring binds it to the current thread via `TransactionSynchronizationManager`) -> if the method returns normally, the proxy calls `connection.commit()`; if it throws an unchecked exception, the proxy calls `connection.rollback()` instead -> the connection's auto-commit is restored and it's returned to the pool.",
        low_level_internals="A CGLIB proxy is a real subclass generated at runteime via bytecode manipulation (ASM) that overrides your method and inserts the transaction-management call before/after invoking `super.yourMethod()` — this is why a `final` class or `final` method cannot be proxied by CGLIB (no subclass possible) and why a `private` method cannot be intercepted (not visible to override). The actual transaction is a real database-level transaction (BEGIN/COMMIT/ROLLBACK at the SQL level, or JDBC's `setAutoCommit(false)`); the JVM-side mechanism is just what decides when to call commit/rollback.",
        who="Spring's AOP proxy factory (choosing JDK dynamic proxy vs. CGLIB), the `PlatformTransactionManager` (deciding begin/commit/rollback), the JDBC driver/connection (executing the actual BEGIN/COMMIT), HikariCP (holding the connection for the transaction's duration), and the database engine (enforcing the actual atomicity).",
        industry="'Why didn't my @Transactional rollback' is a top-tier recurring Spring interview and Stack Overflow question — self-invocation and checked-exception rollback defaults are the two most common causes cited across the industry.",
        business="A transaction that silently fails to apply (self-invocation bug) can leave a multi-step business operation (e.g. inventory decrement + order creation) partially applied, corrupting business data in a way that's very hard to notice until reconciliation.",
        product="Users never see '@Transactional' — they see 'my order says it succeeded but the inventory wasn't updated,' which is this exact failure mode surfacing as a confusing, hard-to-reproduce product bug report.",
        cost_economics="Holding a transaction open for longer than necessary (e.g. including a slow external HTTP call inside a `@Transactional` method) holds a pooled database connection the entire time, directly reducing the effective size of the connection pool available to other concurrent requests — a cheap-looking annotation with a real, easily-overlooked cost under load.",
        failure_incident="Symptom: a method annotated `@Transactional` doesn't roll back when it should, or a caught-and-logged checked exception leaves partial writes committed. Telemetry: partial data (e.g. order row exists, inventory row unchanged) discovered on inspection, no error surfaced to the caller. Hypothesis: either a self-invocation bypassing the proxy, or Spring's default rollback-only-on-unchecked-exception behavior silently not covering a checked exception path. Diagnosis: code review finds the `@Transactional` method called from within the same class (`this.method()`), or a checked exception caught internally without `rollbackFor` specified. Root cause: misunderstanding that the annotation only takes effect through the external proxy call path. Fix: move the method to a different (injected) bean, or explicitly configure `@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)`. Regression: an integration test that verifies rollback actually occurs on the specific failure path, since a proxy-bypass bug is invisible to a test that only checks the happy path.",
        alternatives_tradeoffs="Declarative `@Transactional` (concise, but has proxy-related edge cases) vs. programmatic `TransactionTemplate` (verbose, but explicit and immune to self-invocation issues since there's no proxy to bypass) vs. relying on database-level atomicity alone for single-statement operations (simplest, correct only when the operation genuinely is a single statement).",
        scenario="STUDY_SCENARIO: a `CustomerService.mergeAccounts()` method calls `this.deactivateOldAccount()` internally, itself separately annotated `@Transactional`; because it's a same-class self-invocation, the inner transactional boundary is silently ignored and a partial merge is possible on failure. Diagnosing and explaining this from first principles (proxy mechanism, not memorized trivia) is strong interview material.",
        interview_mode=im(
            sec_15="@Transactional works via a dynamic proxy that wraps your bean, starting a transaction before your method runs and committing or rolling back after, based on the connection Spring binds to the current thread.",
            sec_30="Because it's implemented as a proxy around the bean, calling a @Transactional method from another method in the same class (self-invocation) bypasses the proxy entirely and the transaction silently doesn't apply — this is the single most common real-world bug with this feature, alongside the default of rolling back only on unchecked exceptions.",
            min_2="Explain the CGLIB proxy mechanism concretely, then walk the self-invocation failure scenario end to end as a live diagnosis example, and mention the checked-exception rollback default as a second common trap.",
            deep_technical="Explain why CGLIB can't proxy final classes/methods or intercept private methods (no override point), and why the actual atomicity guarantee ultimately comes from the database transaction, not from Spring itself.",
            system_design="Frame transaction boundary placement (Service layer, one per business operation) as a deliberate architecture decision protecting a specific invariant — no partially-applied business operation is ever visible.",
            business_value="A silently-skipped transaction boundary corrupts business data without any error — this is the kind of bug that erodes trust in a system's data quietly, over time.",
        ),
        current_status_2026="Spring Framework 6 / Boot 3.x (current baseline in 2026) still uses the same AOP-proxy transaction mechanism; AspectJ compile-time weaving remains available as an alternative that avoids the self-invocation trap entirely but is rarely used outside specific performance-sensitive cases due to its build-time complexity.",
    )


