# RELIABILITY / DISTRIBUTED CONSISTENCY / CAPACITY
# ======================================================================

def topic_resilience_retry_timeout_circuit_breaker():
    return itopic(
        "Resilience Patterns: Retry, Timeout & Circuit Breaker", ESSENTIAL, CURRENT,
        short_overview="The three basic tools for surviving a slow or failing dependency without letting its failure become your own outage — and how they compose.",
        why="Every real distributed system has dependencies that will eventually be slow or fail, and how a service behaves in that moment is what separates a resilient architecture from a fragile one that turns one dependency's bad day into a full outage — this is a core System Design and senior-engineering interview topic because it tests judgment about failure, not just happy-path design. This project's own event ledger already implements exactly this reasoning (retry-with-idempotent-fallback, and an explicit timeout on a hanging fetch), making this genuine current project experience, not purely theoretical.",
        history_evolution="Netflix's public engineering writing (especially the Hystrix library, released 2012, and the broader 'Chaos Engineering' practice it grew out of) is the most influential single source popularizing circuit breakers and bulkheads as standard, named patterns for JVM microservices, following real, well-documented cascading-failure incidents at that scale. The circuit-breaker metaphor itself borrows directly from electrical engineering (a physical breaker that trips to stop current flow before it causes further damage). Resilience4j (2017+) succeeded Hystrix (which entered maintenance mode in 2018) as the modern, lightweight, functional-style Java resilience library, now Spring Cloud's recommended default.",
        why_now="Needed wherever a service calls another service or external dependency over a network — which is essentially every real backend system with any external integration. Unnecessary for a purely local, in-process call with no network/failure boundary at all.",
        big_picture="Business requirement: a temporary slowdown or outage in one dependency degrades gracefully rather than cascading into a full platform outage. NFR: bounded worst-case latency (a hung dependency must not hang every caller indefinitely) and fault isolation. Architecture: these patterns sit at every network call boundary — a client library, an API gateway, or a service-mesh sidecar — wrapping the call rather than being scattered ad hoc through business logic.",
        design_intellect="Each pattern protects a distinct invariant: a timeout protects 'a caller never waits forever' (bounding worst-case latency even when the callee never responds at all); a retry (paired with idempotency) protects 'a transient failure doesn't become a permanent one' by giving the operation another chance, ideally with backoff so retries don't themselves worsen an already-struggling dependency; a circuit breaker protects the dependency itself and the caller's own resources by stopping calls entirely for a cooldown period once failures cross a threshold, on the insight that hammering an already-failing dependency with more traffic only makes recovery slower and wastes the caller's own threads/connections waiting on calls very likely to fail anyway.",
        how_exactly="A Service makes a call to a dependency (an HTTP call to another service, or a slow external API) -> a timeout wraps the call so if no response arrives within a bounded window, the call fails fast rather than hanging the calling thread indefinitely -> if the failure is classified as likely-transient (a timeout, a 503, a network blip), a retry policy re-attempts the call, typically with exponential backoff (waiting progressively longer between attempts) and a jitter component (randomizing the exact wait slightly so many callers don't retry in lockstep and create a synchronized retry storm) -> a circuit breaker tracks the failure rate of calls to this dependency over a rolling window; once failures exceed a threshold, it 'opens' and short-circuits all further calls immediately (returning a fast, explicit failure or a fallback) without even attempting the network call, for a cooldown period -> after the cooldown, it moves to 'half-open,' allowing a small number of trial calls through to test whether the dependency has recovered, closing again (resuming normal calls) if they succeed.",
        low_level_internals="A timeout is typically implemented either at the HTTP client level (a socket/connection/read timeout on the underlying `HttpClient`) or, when the underlying call has no built-in timeout mechanism, via a race between the call's own future/promise and a separate timer task, exactly the fix this project applied to its own hanging `fetch()` call. A circuit breaker's state machine (closed -> open -> half-open -> closed) is maintained in-memory per-instance (or shared via a distributed store for consistency across instances) and typically uses a sliding window (count- or time-based) to compute the current failure rate rather than reacting to a single failure.",
        who="The calling service's own client code/library (or a shared sidecar/service-mesh layer, in more centralized architectures), the resilience library (Resilience4j, or hand-rolled logic), and the dependency being called (whose actual health the circuit breaker is protecting both the caller and the dependency itself from over-stressing).",
        industry="Netflix's Hystrix/Resilience4j lineage and the specific vocabulary (circuit breaker, bulkhead, backoff-with-jitter) are assumed baseline knowledge in nearly every senior backend/System Design interview involving any service-to-service communication.",
        business="An unprotected synchronous dependency turning a partial outage into a full cascading outage directly costs revenue and customer trust for the entire duration of the incident, often far longer and broader than the original, smaller failure would have caused alone.",
        product="Users experience the absence of these patterns as the whole app becoming unresponsive when only one, often minor, feature's dependency is actually struggling — degraded, not fully failed, behavior (a feature showing a friendly 'temporarily unavailable' instead of the whole app hanging) is the resilience payoff a product team directly benefits from.",
        cost_economics="These patterns cost a modest amount of engineering complexity (configuration, testing failure paths) in exchange for bounding the blast radius and duration of any single dependency's failure — an unambiguously good trade at any real production scale, and one of the cheapest reliability investments available relative to the outage cost they prevent.",
        failure_incident="Symptom: one slow downstream dependency (e.g. an external payment gateway experiencing a slowdown) causes the entire calling service to become unresponsive, exhausting its own thread/connection pool as every incoming request blocks waiting on the slow dependency. Telemetry: thread pool utilization pinned at maximum, request queue growing, correlated with elevated response times specifically from the one dependency. Hypothesis: no timeout on the outbound call, so slow responses hold calling threads indefinitely instead of failing fast. Diagnosis: thread dump shows most threads blocked in the exact same outbound call stack frame. Root cause: missing timeout (and, compounding it, no circuit breaker to stop attempting calls once the dependency was clearly degraded). Fix: add an explicit timeout, wrap the call in a circuit breaker so once failures cross a threshold, further calls fail fast without consuming a thread at all, and add backoff-with-jitter to any retry. Regression: a test that simulates a slow/failing dependency and asserts the calling service's own thread pool never becomes fully exhausted and continues serving unrelated requests. Business impact: this exact pattern (this project's own real hung-fetch bug, and the classic industry cascading-failure incident shape) turns a single dependency's bad day into the whole platform's outage.",
        alternatives_tradeoffs="Timeout + retry + circuit breaker used together (the standard, layered approach — each protects a different failure dimension) vs. retry alone without a circuit breaker (risks a retry storm making an already-struggling dependency worse) vs. a circuit breaker without a bulkhead (isolated failure detection, but a slow dependency can still exhaust shared thread/connection resources before the breaker even trips) vs. a bulkhead (isolating a limited pool of resources per-dependency so one dependency's exhaustion can't starve calls to unrelated dependencies, a natural complement to a circuit breaker at larger scale).",
        scenario=None,
        interview_mode=im(
            sec_15="Timeout bounds how long a caller waits for a possibly-hung dependency; retry (with backoff/jitter) gives a transient failure another chance without worsening the problem; a circuit breaker stops calling an already-failing dependency for a cooldown period so it isn't hammered while recovering and so the caller's own resources aren't wasted on calls unlikely to succeed.",
            sec_30="These three patterns protect different things: timeout bounds the caller's own worst-case latency, retry recovers from transient failures (only safe when paired with idempotency), and a circuit breaker protects both the struggling dependency and the caller's resources by failing fast once failures cross a threshold, resuming gradually via a half-open trial state.",
            min_2="Use this project's own real incident (a hanging fetch() with no built-in timeout, fixed with an explicit Promise.race timeout) as the concrete opening example, then generalize to the full pattern trio and walk the classic thread-pool-exhaustion cascading-failure scenario end to end.",
            deep_technical="Explain the circuit breaker's state machine precisely (closed/open/half-open) and why the half-open trial state exists (to detect recovery without either staying open forever or fully reopening traffic before confirming the dependency is actually healthy).",
            system_design="Position these patterns as living at every network call boundary in the architecture diagram, not as an afterthought bolted on after an incident — a mature System Design answer treats failure handling as a first-class design input from the start.",
            incident_debugging="Cite the specific diagnostic signature: a thread dump showing most threads blocked in the identical outbound-call stack frame is the classic fingerprint of a missing timeout causing cascading exhaustion.",
        ),
        evidence=["agent/event_ledger.py", "docs/LESSONS.md"],
        current_status_2026="Resilience4j remains the standard Java/Spring resilience library in 2026; service-mesh solutions (Istio, Linkerd) increasingly implement these same patterns (timeout, retry, circuit breaking) transparently at the infrastructure/sidecar layer rather than in application code, an important current architectural option worth mentioning alongside the classic library-level approach.",
    )


def topic_distributed_consistency_saga_outbox():
    return itopic(
        "Distributed Transactions: Saga & Outbox Patterns", MASTERY, STUDY,
        short_overview="Why a single ACID transaction can't span multiple services, and the two standard patterns (Saga, Outbox) that achieve consistency anyway.",
        why="Once a business operation spans more than one service (each owning its own database, per correct microservice boundaries), a single classic ACID transaction across all of them is no longer possible — this is one of the more advanced, staff-level System Design interview topics because it requires understanding both why the naive solution (two-phase commit) is rarely used in practice and what the accepted real-world alternatives actually are.",
        history_evolution="Distributed two-phase commit (2PC, formalized in the 1970s-80s alongside classic transaction theory) is the theoretically 'correct' way to coordinate a transaction across multiple resource managers, but it blocks all participants while waiting on a coordinator and does not tolerate the coordinator failing mid-protocol — properties that make it a poor fit for internet-scale, independently-deployed, unreliable-network microservices, which is why it's rarely used in modern distributed architectures despite being the first thing many candidates reach for. The Saga pattern (first described in a 1987 database systems paper by Garcia-Molina and Salem, long before microservices existed, then rediscovered and popularized for microservices in the mid-2010s) instead breaks a distributed transaction into a sequence of local transactions, each with a defined compensating action to undo it if a later step fails. The Transactional Outbox pattern (popularized alongside Debezium and change-data-capture tooling in the mid-2010s) solves a narrower but ubiquitous related problem: reliably publishing an event as part of a local database transaction.",
        why_now="Needed specifically when a single business operation must update data owned by more than one independently-deployed service and the business requires it to end up fully consistent (all steps applied, or all effectively undone) despite no single database transaction being able to span both. Unnecessary complexity for anything that fits inside one service's own database transaction.",
        big_picture="Business requirement: a multi-step business process (e.g. 'place an order' touching Order, Inventory, and Payment services) either fully completes or is fully, correctly unwound if any step fails partway — without ever locking resources across services for the duration (which 2PC would require). Architecture: an orchestrated or choreographed sequence of local transactions, each independently committed, with explicit compensating actions defined for every step that might need to be undone.",
        design_intellect="The core idea of Saga is trading atomicity for eventual consistency plus explicit compensation: instead of 'all steps happen as one indivisible unit or none happen at all' (impossible across independently-owned databases without blocking coordination), each step commits independently and immediately, and if a later step fails, previously-completed steps are undone by running a separate, explicitly-authored compensating transaction (e.g. 'refund the payment' as the compensation for 'charge the payment'). The Outbox pattern's core idea is narrower: write the business change and the 'event to be published' row to the same local database transaction (so they're atomically consistent with each other by construction), then have a separate, reliable process (a poller, or change-data-capture reading the database's write-ahead log) actually publish that event to the broker — this avoids the classic dual-write problem, where writing to a database and then separately publishing to a message broker can succeed at one and fail at the other, leaving them inconsistent.",
        how_exactly="An order-placement Saga begins -> the Order service commits a local transaction creating the order in a 'pending' state -> it then triggers (via orchestration, a central coordinator calling each step, or choreography, each service reacting to the previous step's event) the Inventory service, which commits its own local transaction reserving stock -> then the Payment service commits its own local transaction charging the customer -> if the Payment step fails, the Saga's compensation logic runs in reverse: Inventory's compensating transaction releases the reserved stock, and Order's compensating transaction marks the order as failed/cancelled -> at no point does any single transaction span more than one service's database; consistency across the whole sequence is achieved by the guaranteed eventual execution of either every forward step or every needed compensation.",
        low_level_internals="An orchestrated Saga uses a central coordinator service that explicitly calls each participant and tracks Saga state (which steps have completed, which compensations are pending) — simpler to reason about and debug, at the cost of a central component. A choreographed Saga instead has each service publish an event on completing its own step, with the next service(s) subscribing and reacting — more decoupled, but harder to trace end-to-end since there's no single place the whole sequence's logic lives. The Outbox pattern's reliability specifically comes from the outbox table's row being written inside the exact same local database transaction as the business change, so a database crash after that commit can never lose the fact that the event still needs publishing — a separate poller or CDC process reads unpublished outbox rows and publishes them, retrying until acknowledged, then marks them published.",
        who="Each participating service's own local database and transaction manager, a Saga orchestrator (in the orchestrated variant) or each service's own event-publishing logic (choreographed variant), the outbox poller/CDC process (Debezium is the most common real-world implementation), and the message broker carrying Saga step/compensation events.",
        industry="Saga and Outbox are considered staff/principal-level System Design vocabulary specifically for distributed-transaction questions; interviewers commonly ask 'why not just use 2PC' to test whether a candidate understands 2PC's real operational drawbacks (blocking, coordinator single point of failure) rather than reflexively citing it as 'the correct' distributed-transaction answer.",
        business="Getting compensation logic wrong (e.g. failing to actually release reserved inventory on a failed order) directly corrupts business data (inventory permanently reserved for a cancelled order) — Saga's correctness depends entirely on every compensating action being genuinely, robustly implemented, which is real, non-trivial engineering work, not a free byproduct of the pattern's name.",
        product="A correctly-implemented Saga is invisible to the user beyond perhaps a slightly longer perceived completion time for a multi-step action; a poorly-implemented one surfaces as orders stuck in a permanently 'processing' state, or as data that's visibly inconsistent across different parts of the product for a period of time (eventual, not immediate, consistency).",
        cost_economics="Saga/Outbox add real engineering cost (every step needs an explicit, tested compensating action; the outbox needs its own polling/CDC infrastructure) that's only justified once a business process genuinely spans multiple independently-owned services — for anything that fits in one service's database, this complexity is pure unjustified overhead.",
        failure_incident="Symptom: a cancelled order leaves inventory permanently reserved, never released back to available stock. Telemetry: inventory reserved-but-unsold count grows over time, correlated with failed/cancelled orders. Hypothesis: the Saga's compensating transaction for the Inventory step either wasn't implemented, wasn't triggered correctly, or itself failed silently. Diagnosis: Saga execution logs (or event history) show the compensation event was never published, or was published but the Inventory service never processed it. Root cause: an incomplete or unreliable compensation path — the classic Saga implementation gap. Fix: implement (or fix) the compensating transaction, and specifically make the compensation trigger itself durable/retryable (ironically, often needing the same Outbox-pattern reliability applied to the compensation event as to the original one). Regression: a test that deliberately fails the Payment step and asserts Inventory's reservation is provably released afterward. Business impact: silently unusable inventory (a real, hidden cost) that accumulates invisibly until a manual reconciliation eventually surfaces it.",
        alternatives_tradeoffs="Orchestrated Saga (centralized, easier to trace/debug, a coordinator is a new component to build and keep available) vs. choreographed Saga (fully decentralized, no single point of failure, but end-to-end behavior is harder to see in one place, and adding a new participant later can require touching multiple existing services' event-listening logic) vs. 2PC (theoretically simpler mental model, strong consistency, but blocking and poor failure tolerance — rarely chosen in modern practice) vs. avoiding the problem entirely by keeping the whole operation inside one service's database transaction (only possible if the service boundaries genuinely allow it — often the better fix is reconsidering the boundary itself, per Microservice Boundaries).",
        scenario="STUDY_SCENARIO: an order-placement flow spans Order, Inventory, and Payment services; a payment failure must trigger inventory release and order cancellation as compensations. Walking through both the orchestrated and choreographed variants, and specifically the failure mode where a compensation itself fails to run reliably (leaving permanently-reserved inventory), demonstrates staff-level System Design depth even without this exact system having been built.",
        interview_mode=im(
            sec_15="A Saga breaks a distributed transaction into a sequence of local transactions, each with an explicit compensating action to undo it if a later step fails — trading a single all-or-nothing transaction (impossible across independently-owned service databases) for eventual consistency plus guaranteed compensation.",
            sec_30="Two-phase commit is the theoretically 'correct' distributed transaction protocol but blocks all participants and doesn't tolerate coordinator failure well, which is why real microservice architectures use Saga instead — either orchestrated (a central coordinator calls each step) or choreographed (each service reacts to the previous step's event) — paired with the Outbox pattern to reliably publish each step's event as part of its own local transaction.",
            min_2="Explain why 2PC is rarely used in practice, walk a concrete order-placement Saga with a payment failure and its inventory-release/order-cancellation compensations, and mention the Outbox pattern as the mechanism that makes each step's event publication reliable despite the classic dual-write problem.",
            deep_technical="Explain the dual-write problem precisely (writing to a database and separately publishing to a broker can succeed at one and fail at the other) and how the Outbox pattern's same-transaction write eliminates it.",
            system_design="This is exactly the kind of topic to bring up proactively when a System Design interview's proposed architecture has multiple services each needing to react atomically to one business event — showing awareness that 2PC isn't the practical answer here signals real depth.",
            business_value="Emphasize that Saga's correctness lives entirely in the compensating actions being genuinely robust — an unimplemented or unreliable compensation silently corrupts business data, a real engineering responsibility, not a solved-by-the-pattern-name guarantee.",
        ),
        current_status_2026="Debezium (change-data-capture, reading a database's write-ahead log to reliably implement the Outbox pattern without a custom poller) remains the standard open-source tool for this in 2026; Saga orchestration frameworks (e.g. Temporal, a durable-workflow-execution engine) have become an increasingly popular alternative to hand-rolling Saga coordination logic, treating the whole multi-step, compensatable business process as a durable, resumable program rather than a set of hand-wired event listeners.",
    )


def topic_system_design_capacity_estimation():
    return itopic(
        "System Design: Capacity Estimation & Back-of-Envelope Math", ESSENTIAL, STUDY,
        short_overview="The concrete arithmetic (QPS, storage, bandwidth) that turns a vague 'design X at scale' prompt into a specific, defensible architecture.",
        why="Nearly every System Design interview explicitly rewards doing real, if approximate, capacity math before proposing an architecture — it's the single clearest signal separating a candidate who can justify their design choices with numbers from one who's pattern-matching to a memorized architecture diagram regardless of the actual scale implied by the prompt.",
        history_evolution="'Back-of-envelope' estimation as a named engineering practice traces to physicist Enrico Fermi's famous habit of estimating physical quantities (like the Trinity test's blast yield, from paper drifting in the shockwave) using only rough numbers and reasoning chains — the same 'Fermi estimation' approach was adopted into software capacity planning as systems grew large enough that provisioning decisions needed justification before being built. It became a standard, explicit part of the tech-industry System Design interview format as those interviews formalized in the 2010s (popularized in written form by resources like 'System Design Interview' by Alex Xu, 2020).",
        why_now="Needed the moment a design decision (how many database replicas, whether to shard, whether caching is even necessary) depends on actual expected load — skipping this step and jumping straight to 'we'll use microservices and Kafka' regardless of whether the system serves 10 or 10 million users per day is a common, easily-avoided interview and real-world mistake.",
        big_picture="Business requirement: the proposed architecture is sized to the business's actual (or realistically projected) scale — neither so small it will fall over immediately, nor so large it wastes budget solving a problem the business doesn't have yet. NFR: explicit, numeric targets for QPS, latency, storage growth, and bandwidth, derived from stated or reasonably-assumed business inputs (daily active users, actions per user, average payload size), not left as vague adjectives.",
        design_intellect="The core idea is that a small number of derived numbers (average QPS, peak QPS via a peak-to-average multiplier, storage growth per year, bandwidth) determine which architectural techniques are actually necessary — caching is only worth discussing once read QPS is shown to be high; sharding is only worth discussing once single-node storage or write-throughput limits are shown to be approached; a load balancer is only meaningfully justified once a single server's estimated capacity is shown to be insufficient for peak QPS. Deriving these numbers first, then letting them drive the design, is what separates principled System Design from architecture-by-buzzword.",
        how_exactly="Given a stated or assumed business input (e.g. '10 million daily active users, each performs 5 actions/day') -> total daily actions = DAU x actions/day (50 million) -> average QPS = total daily actions / 86,400 seconds (~580 QPS) -> peak QPS is estimated by applying a peaking factor (commonly 2-3x average, adjusted for known traffic patterns like time-zone concentration or event-driven spikes) giving a peak design target (~1,200-1,700 QPS) -> storage estimate: (average payload size per action) x (total actions per day) x (retention period in days) gives raw storage growth, which then informs replication factor and database/partitioning strategy -> these numbers then directly justify (or rule out) specific techniques: e.g. a single well-provisioned database instance can often handle far more QPS than candidates assume, meaning premature sharding is a common over-engineering mistake revealed by actually doing the math.",
        low_level_internals="These estimates connect directly to real hardware limits worth knowing approximate orders of magnitude for: a single modern SSD read is on the order of ~100 microseconds; an L1 cache reference ~1 nanosecond, RAM reference ~100 nanoseconds, a same-datacenter network round trip ~0.5 millisecond, a cross-continent round trip ~100-150 milliseconds (the widely-cited 'latency numbers every programmer should know,' originally compiled by Jeff Dean and Peter Norvig at Google) — knowing these orders of magnitude is what lets a candidate sanity-check whether a proposed design's latency budget is even physically plausible.",
        who="The System Design candidate/engineer doing the estimation, informed by realistic assumptions about the business (DAU, actions per user, payload sizes) either given in the prompt or explicitly stated as an assumption, since a wrong-but-explicitly-stated assumption is far better than an unstated one.",
        industry="Every major tech company's System Design interview format explicitly allocates time for and rewards capacity estimation before architecture proposal; it's considered a baseline, table-stakes skill for mid-to-senior backend roles, not an advanced/optional topic.",
        business="Correctly-sized capacity planning directly controls infrastructure cost — provisioning for an imagined 'web scale' when actual traffic is orders of magnitude smaller wastes real money every month, while under-provisioning risks an outage at exactly the moment of business success (a product going viral, a marketing campaign landing).",
        product="A product's actual usage pattern (bursty vs. steady, read-heavy vs. write-heavy) directly determines which parts of a design need investment — capacity estimation is the bridge between 'what the product does' and 'what the system must be built to handle.'",
        cost_economics="This entire topic is fundamentally a cost-optimization discipline: every architectural technique (caching, sharding, replication, CDN) has a real infrastructure and complexity cost, and capacity estimation is what justifies (or rules out) paying that cost for a given system's actual scale.",
        failure_incident="Symptom: a proposed System Design in an interview (or a real architecture in production) includes complex scaling techniques (sharding, multi-region replication) with no numeric justification, and separately fails to account for a specific bottleneck (e.g. a single component's realistic single-node capacity) that the actual estimated load would have revealed. Diagnosis (interview context): the interviewer probes 'how many requests per second do you expect this to handle' and the candidate has no answer, or the design doesn't change based on the answer once given. Root cause: skipped or superficial capacity estimation. Fix: always derive QPS/storage/bandwidth numbers early, from stated or explicitly-assumed inputs, and let those numbers drive (and be visibly referenced by) every subsequent design decision. Business impact (real-world): either wasted infrastructure spend from unjustified over-engineering, or a genuine capacity-driven outage from under-provisioning that correct estimation would have flagged in advance.",
        alternatives_tradeoffs="Rigorous, stated-assumption-based estimation (defensible, shows real engineering judgment, takes a few minutes of interview time) vs. skipping estimation and defaulting to a 'standard' scalable architecture regardless of actual scale (faster, but reveals pattern-matching rather than reasoning, and is a common way candidates lose credibility in a System Design interview) vs. over-precise estimation chasing exact figures (wastes interview time on decimal-point precision when the actual goal is correct order-of-magnitude reasoning).",
        scenario=None,
        interview_mode=im(
            sec_15="Before proposing an architecture, derive rough average and peak QPS, storage growth, and bandwidth from the given (or explicitly assumed) business scale — these numbers should visibly drive which techniques (caching, sharding, load balancing) are actually justified.",
            sec_30="State assumptions explicitly (DAU, actions per user, payload size), derive average QPS by dividing daily actions by seconds-per-day, apply a peaking factor for the design target, and estimate storage growth from payload size times daily volume times retention — then reference these numbers when justifying every subsequent architecture decision, rather than defaulting to a fixed 'scalable' pattern regardless of actual scale.",
            min_2="Walk a full example (e.g. 10M DAU, 5 actions/day) through average QPS, peak QPS, and storage estimation, explicitly stating each assumption, then show how the resulting numbers determine whether caching/sharding/a CDN are actually justified for this specific scale versus overkill.",
            deep_technical="Cite the 'latency numbers every programmer should know' (L1 cache ~1ns, RAM ~100ns, SSD read ~100us, same-datacenter round trip ~0.5ms, cross-continent round trip ~100-150ms) to sanity-check whether a proposed design's latency budget is physically plausible.",
            system_design="This is the opening move of nearly every System Design interview — do it first, explicitly, before any component diagram, and revisit the numbers whenever proposing a scaling technique to justify it's actually needed at this scale.",
            business_value="Frame over-engineering (unjustified complexity for the actual scale) and under-provisioning (an outage at the worst possible moment) as two symmetric, equally real cost mistakes that correct estimation avoids.",
        ),
        current_status_2026="The core Fermi-estimation methodology and the 'latency numbers every programmer should know' reference figures remain unchanged and are still the standard baseline cited in System Design interviews and preparation material in 2026; what has shifted is that many candidates now also need to estimate AI/LLM-inference-related capacity (tokens/sec, context-window memory, GPU/accelerator throughput) using the same fundamental estimation discipline applied to a newer class of resource.",
    )


