def topic_jwt_oauth_spring_security():
    return itopic(
        "Authentication & Authorization: JWT, OAuth2/OIDC & Spring Security", ESSENTIAL, STUDY,
        short_overview="The distinction between proving who you are and deciding what you're allowed to do, and the standard protocols/tokens that implement each at scale.",
        why="Authentication and authorization are foundational to essentially every real backend system with more than one user, and are consistently tested in backend interviews both as concrete protocol knowledge (JWT structure, OAuth2 flows) and as a System Design topic (how to secure service-to-service calls). This project's own Customer app has explicitly not yet implemented an auth layer, making this honestly STUDY_SCENARIO/general knowledge rather than demonstrated project experience.",
        history_evolution="HTTP Basic Auth (part of the original HTTP spec, 1990s) and server-side sessions (a session ID cookie referencing server-stored state) were the original web auth mechanisms — sessions require server-side state, which becomes awkward once an API must scale horizontally across many stateless servers or be consumed by non-browser clients (mobile apps, other services). OAuth 2.0 (finalized as RFC 6749, 2012, led by the IETF working group with heavy industry input from Google, Twitter, Facebook) standardized delegated authorization (letting an app act on a user's behalf without ever seeing their password) originally for third-party API access. JWT (JSON Web Token, RFC 7519, 2015) provided a compact, self-contained, cryptographically signed token format that let a server verify a token's authenticity without a database lookup — a natural fit for OAuth2's access tokens and for stateless API authentication generally. OpenID Connect (OIDC, 2014) layered a standardized *authentication* protocol (proving identity) on top of OAuth2's *authorization* framework, since OAuth2 itself was never actually designed to answer 'who is this user,' only 'what can this token do' — a frequently confused distinction even among practitioners.",
        why_now="Authentication/authorization is needed the moment a system has more than one type of user or must restrict any action to specific identities — essentially every real product. JWTs specifically are justified when statelessness (no server-side session lookup needed to validate a request) and cross-service verification (any service holding the signing key/public key can verify a token independently) are valuable, such as in a microservices architecture; a simple server-side session is often simpler and sufficient for a single monolithic application with no cross-service verification need.",
        big_picture="Business requirement: only the right identity can perform a given action, and that identity's authenticity can be verified cheaply and reliably on every request. NFR: authentication/authorization must not become a latency or availability bottleneck (a JWT's self-contained verification specifically avoids a database round trip on every single request, unlike a naive session-lookup design). Architecture: authentication (login, token issuance) typically happens once at a dedicated identity provider/auth server; authorization (checking a token's claims/scopes against what's needed for a specific action) happens on every subsequent request, ideally cheaply, at each service boundary.",
        design_intellect="The core idea of a JWT is a self-contained, tamper-evident claim: a signed token carries its own claims (user ID, roles/scopes, expiry) such that any party holding the corresponding verification key can confirm the token is authentic and unmodified without needing to ask the issuing server again — trading a small amount of token size (claims travel with every request) for eliminating a database/session-store round trip on every request. The invariant protected is that a token's claims cannot be forged or altered without invalidating its signature, which is exactly why a JWT's payload, while not encrypted by default, is safe to trust for authorization decisions but must never contain secrets meant to stay hidden from the token holder.",
        how_exactly="A user submits credentials to an authorization server (or a service acting as one) -> upon successful verification, the server issues a signed JWT access token containing claims (subject/user ID, roles or scopes, issued-at and expiry timestamps) -> the client includes this token on every subsequent request, typically as an `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header -> a receiving service's security filter (in Spring, a `SecurityFilterChain`/`OncePerRequestFilter`) intercepts the request before it reaches any Controller, extracts the token, verifies its signature against the known public key/shared secret (a fast, local cryptographic check — no network call needed), and checks its expiry -> if valid, the service extracts the claims and populates the request's security context (who this is, what they're allowed to do) -> Spring Security's method-level authorization (`@PreAuthorize`) or the Controller logic then checks those claims against what the specific action requires -> if the token is invalid, expired, or lacks the required scope, the request is rejected (401 or 403) before business logic ever runs.",
        low_level_internals="A JWT is three base64url-encoded segments joined by dots: a header (algorithm, e.g. `HS256`/`RS256`), a payload (the claims, human-readable once decoded — never assume a JWT's contents are hidden from the holder, only that they're tamper-evident), and a signature (an HMAC over the header+payload using a shared secret, for `HS256`, or an RSA/ECDSA signature using a private key with a publicly-distributable verification key, for `RS256` — `RS256` is generally preferred across service boundaries since it lets any service verify tokens using only a public key, without ever holding the secret that could forge one). Verification is a local, offline cryptographic computation (no network call), which is precisely the performance property that makes JWTs attractive for high-throughput, multi-service systems compared to a session-store lookup on every request.",
        who="The identity/authorization server (issuing and signing tokens), the client (storing and attaching the token to requests), each resource server's security filter (verifying signatures and extracting claims on every request), and, in an OIDC flow specifically, an identity provider distinct from the resource servers that ultimately consume the resulting tokens.",
        industry="JWT/OAuth2/OIDC knowledge, and specifically the ability to explain the distinction between authentication and authorization precisely, is assumed baseline knowledge for any backend role and is frequently probed via 'walk me through what happens when a user logs in and then makes an authenticated API call' style questions.",
        business="A broken or missing authorization check (as opposed to authentication) is one of the most common and severe classes of real security incident — a user being correctly authenticated as themselves but able to access another user's data due to a missing per-resource authorization check (an IDOR, insecure direct object reference) is a top-tier real-world vulnerability class, distinct from and more subtle than simply 'not requiring login at all.'",
        product="Auth failures are maximally user-visible and trust-damaging when they go wrong in either direction — locking legitimate users out, or (far worse) letting a user see or modify data that isn't theirs.",
        cost_economics="JWTs trade token size (larger than a simple session ID, since claims travel with every request) and the inability to instantly revoke a single already-issued token (it remains valid until its stated expiry, since there's no central lookup at verification time) against eliminating a database/session-store round trip on every single request — worth it at scale, and specifically why short expiry times plus a refresh-token flow are the standard mitigation for the revocation limitation.",
        failure_incident="Symptom: a user can retrieve another user's private data by simply changing a resource ID in an otherwise-authenticated API request (e.g. `GET /orders/12345` returning someone else's order data when requested by a different, legitimately-logged-in user). Telemetry: the request succeeds with a 200 and valid-looking data despite the requesting user's ID not matching the resource's owner. Hypothesis: the endpoint correctly authenticates (verifies the JWT is valid) but never checks authorization (whether *this specific* authenticated user is allowed to access *this specific* resource). Diagnosis: code review confirms the Controller/Service checks token validity but not resource ownership. Root cause: authentication and authorization were conflated — a valid token was treated as sufficient for any action, rather than gating each specific resource access against the token's actual identity/claims. Fix: add an explicit per-resource ownership/scope check before returning or modifying any resource. Regression: a test asserting that User A's valid token cannot retrieve User B's resource, added specifically for every resource-owning endpoint. Business impact: a real data breach — one of the most damaging and reputation-costly categories of security incident, and one of the OWASP Top 10 (Broken Access Control).",
        alternatives_tradeoffs="Server-side sessions (simple, instantly revocable, requires server-side state and doesn't scale as cleanly across many stateless services) vs. JWT access tokens (stateless, fast to verify, scales well across services, harder to revoke instantly — mitigated with short expiry + refresh tokens) vs. opaque tokens validated via a call to a central token-introspection endpoint (revocable instantly like a session, but reintroduces a network round trip on every verification, a deliberate trade toward revocability over the stateless-verification performance benefit).",
        scenario="STUDY_SCENARIO: an API correctly requires a valid JWT for every request but never checks that the authenticated user's ID matches the resource being requested, allowing any logged-in user to read any other user's order by simply changing the ID in the URL (a textbook Broken Access Control / IDOR vulnerability). Explaining the precise distinction between authentication succeeding and authorization being entirely missing, and the specific per-resource ownership check that fixes it, is strong, realistic security interview material.",
        interview_mode=im(
            sec_15="Authentication proves who you are (login, token issuance); authorization decides what an already-authenticated identity is allowed to do — JWTs are a common way to carry the result of authentication (as signed claims) so authorization checks on later requests don't need a database lookup.",
            sec_30="A JWT is a signed, self-contained token whose claims can be verified locally (via signature check) without a network call, making it well-suited to stateless, multi-service architectures, at the cost of not being instantly revocable — mitigated with short expiry and refresh tokens. Authorization must still be checked explicitly per resource/action; a valid token alone only proves identity, not permission.",
            min_2="Explain the auth-vs-authz distinction precisely, walk the JWT verification flow (signature check, claims extraction, no network call), then use the IDOR/Broken Access Control incident (valid token, but no per-resource ownership check) as the concrete failure example, since it's the most common and instructive real-world way this whole area actually breaks.",
            deep_technical="Explain the difference between HS256 (shared-secret HMAC, both signing and verifying parties must hold the same secret) and RS256 (asymmetric, only the issuer holds the private signing key, any service can verify with the public key) and why RS256 is generally preferred across service boundaries.",
            system_design="Bring up short-lived access tokens plus longer-lived refresh tokens as the standard System Design answer to JWT's revocation limitation.",
            business_value="Emphasize Broken Access Control (OWASP Top 10) as one of the most damaging real vulnerability classes, precisely because authentication can be entirely correct while authorization is silently missing.",
            incident_debugging="Cite the specific diagnostic signature: a 200 response with correct-looking data for a resource the requesting authenticated user doesn't actually own — the fingerprint of missing per-resource authorization, not a broken login.",
        ),
        current_status_2026="OAuth 2.1 (a consolidating, security-hardening revision of OAuth2's various RFCs, still finalizing as a formal spec but already reflected in current library defaults as of 2025-2026) removes several less-secure legacy flows (notably the Implicit flow) and mandates PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange) even for confidential clients, reflecting hard-won lessons about token-interception vulnerabilities in the original OAuth2 spec. Passkeys/WebAuthn (passwordless, public-key-based authentication) have become an increasingly mainstream authentication method alongside, not replacing, the OAuth2/OIDC/JWT authorization layer described above.",
    )


