def topic_ai_context_engineering_tokenization():
    return itopic(
        "AI Context Engineering & Tokenization", ESSENTIAL, CURRENT,
        short_overview="How a business requirement becomes tokens an LLM actually processes, why context is a finite, costly resource, and how deliberately managing it changes an agent's cost, speed, and correctness.",
        why="An AI coding agent's quality is bounded as much by what's actually in its context window (the right files, the right prior results, not an overwhelming irrelevant dump) as by the underlying model's raw capability — context engineering is the emerging, genuinely distinct discipline of deliberately constructing that context, and it directly determines cost (tokens billed), latency (time to first useful output), and correctness (whether the model has what it needs, and isn't distracted by what it doesn't). This project's own retrieval/RAG index, Skills system, and subagent isolation are real, current project experience of exactly this discipline.",
        history_evolution="Early LLM usage (2020-2022, GPT-3 era) treated 'prompting' as the whole discipline — crafting a single good instruction string. As context windows grew (from ~2K-4K tokens in 2020-era models to 128K-1M+ tokens in 2024-2026 frontier models) and agentic, multi-step, tool-using workflows became practical, it became clear that *what surrounds* the instruction (retrieved documents, prior tool results, conversation history, system instructions) dominates real-world quality far more than instruction phrasing alone — 'context engineering' emerged as an explicit term (widely popularized in agent-building discussions through 2024-2025) to name this shift from crafting a prompt to architecting an entire information environment for the model.",
        why_now="Essential the moment an AI system must work with more information than fits comfortably and cheaply in one context window, or must maintain useful state across many sequential steps (an agent loop) without re-explaining everything from scratch each time — this project's own multi-subagent architecture (isolating a fork's or fresh agent's context from the parent's, deliberately, to avoid polluting the coordinator's context with noise it doesn't need) is a direct real answer to exactly this problem.",
        big_picture="Business requirement: an AI-assisted change is delivered correctly, quickly, and at a defensible cost — all three of which context engineering directly controls. NFR: cost per verified change and time per verified change (Section 8/15's own explicit metrics) are functions of how much irrelevant context is included (wasted tokens, wasted latency) and how much relevant context is missing (causing wrong output, requiring rework). Architecture: retrieval (fetching only the specifically relevant files/knowledge, this project's own RAG index over the repository), task decomposition (a subagent gets a narrow, self-contained brief rather than the entire parent conversation), and explicit context boundaries (a fork inherits full context when that's genuinely useful; a fresh agent starts clean when the parent's history would only be noise).",
        design_intellect="The core idea is that a model's attention and reasoning quality degrade, and its cost/latency strictly increase, as context grows — so the design goal is never 'include everything, just in case' but 'include exactly what's needed for this specific step, structured so the model can use it efficiently.' The invariant this protects is a favorable cost/quality curve: a well-scoped context produces a correct result cheaply and quickly; the identical task with a bloated, unfocused context can produce a worse result while costing far more, because irrelevant content both costs tokens directly and can measurably distract the model from what actually matters.",
        how_exactly="A business requirement is stated -> it's turned into a concrete specification/acceptance contract (this project's own agent/acceptance_contract.py: what observable effect must be true when done) -> relevant repository context is retrieved (via grep/glob/RAG search for the specific files actually relevant to this requirement, not the whole repository) -> this assembled context, plus the specification, is tokenized (broken into subword units by the model's tokenizer) and sent as the model's input -> the transformer processes these input tokens through many layers of attention (each token's representation updated based on its relationship to every other token in context) to predict the next output token, repeated autoregressively to generate a response -> if the response includes a tool call (e.g. 'read this file,' 'run this test'), that call is executed by an external program/process outside the model entirely, and its real result is fed back into the context for the next inference step -> this loop (infer, act, observe, infer again) continues until the task is verifiably complete, with the total token count across every step (input tokens re-sent each turn, plus output tokens generated) directly determining the run's total cost and, combined with model throughput, its total latency.",
        low_level_internals="A tokenizer (typically a byte-pair-encoding or similar subword algorithm) maps text to a sequence of integer token IDs from a fixed vocabulary — common English words are often one token, while rare words/code identifiers/non-English text can split into several tokens, which is why token count doesn't map linearly to character or word count and why code-heavy or non-English content can cost noticeably more tokens per unit of actual content. Each inference step re-processes the entire current context (unless the underlying serving infrastructure uses prompt caching, which reuses computation for an unchanged context prefix across turns, a real and significant latency/cost optimization this project's own session model explicitly relies on for multi-turn conversations). Input tokens and output tokens are typically billed (and computed) at different rates, with output tokens generally markedly more expensive per token since they require one full forward pass through the model per token generated (autoregressive generation), while input tokens can be processed largely in parallel.",
        who="The requester/specification author (human or upstream agent, defining the actual requirement), a context-retrieval mechanism (grep/glob/RAG, deciding what's relevant), the model's tokenizer and transformer (the actual inference engine), the tool-execution layer (real external processes — a shell, a file system, a test runner — that the model itself never directly touches), and, for a multi-agent system, the parent/coordinator agent (deciding what context to share with versus withhold from each subagent).",
        industry="Context engineering is one of the fastest-growing named disciplines in AI-assisted software engineering as of 2025-2026, with major AI lab and tooling blog posts (Anthropic, and various agent-framework vendors) explicitly distinguishing it from 'prompt engineering' as a broader, more architecturally significant practice — increasingly a named skill on senior AI-engineering job postings.",
        business="Poorly-engineered context (dumping an entire repository, or an entire long conversation history, into every single step) directly and often dramatically inflates the token cost and latency of every AI-assisted change, while frequently *reducing* correctness — a double cost with no offsetting benefit, making context engineering a direct lever on both AI-development cost and quality simultaneously.",
        product="For any AI-powered product feature (not just internal tooling), context engineering quality directly determines the user-visible response time and the perceived correctness/relevance of the AI's output — the same discipline used to build this project's own agent is the exact discipline a customer-facing AI product feature needs.",
        cost_economics="Because output tokens generally cost more per token than input tokens, and because context re-sent every turn compounds across a multi-step agent loop, cost per verified change is highly sensitive to both how much unnecessary context is carried forward and how many total inference turns a task requires — deliberately scoping context and using prompt caching where the underlying infrastructure supports it are two of the highest-leverage cost levers available in agentic AI development, comparable in importance to the JDBC connection-pool-sizing lesson: bigger/more isn't automatically better.",
        failure_incident="Symptom: an AI agent's response to a simple, narrow task is unexpectedly slow and expensive, or gives an off-target answer that seems to have been distracted by unrelated information. Telemetry: the run's token count (visible via this project's own event ledger/session-history capture) is far higher than the task's apparent complexity would suggest, dominated by input tokens rather than output. Hypothesis: the context assembled for this task included large amounts of irrelevant material (an entire file when only one function mattered, an entire prior conversation when only its final decision mattered). Diagnosis: inspecting the actual context sent (this project's own captured prompt/context summaries) confirms significant irrelevant content. Root cause: retrieval/context-assembly logic wasn't scoped tightly enough to the specific task at hand. Fix: narrow the retrieval to the specific files/facts genuinely relevant (better grep/glob targeting, or summarizing rather than including raw prior history), and, for a repeated subtask, consider a dedicated subagent with a deliberately narrow, self-contained brief instead of inheriting a large parent context. Regression: tracking token-count-per-task-type over time (this project's own economics/session-history intent) to catch a regression in context-assembly efficiency before it becomes a large recurring cost. Business impact: directly inflated AI-development cost and, often worse, degraded output quality, for the exact same underlying task.",
        alternatives_tradeoffs="Include everything potentially relevant (safest against missing something the model needs, most expensive and slowest, and risks quality degradation from distraction) vs. aggressively minimal, tightly-retrieved context (cheapest and fastest, risks missing something genuinely needed, causing a wrong answer and costly rework) vs. a fresh subagent with a narrow, self-contained brief (isolates a subtask's context cleanly, at the cost of losing the parent's accumulated context entirely, which is only free when that subtask genuinely doesn't need it) vs. a forked agent inheriting full parent context (preserves everything, appropriate when the follow-on task genuinely needs the full history, wasteful when it doesn't).",
        scenario=None,
        interview_mode=im(
            sec_15="Context engineering is deliberately constructing exactly the information (retrieved files, prior results, specification) an AI model needs for a specific step — no more, no less — because both cost and output quality are highly sensitive to what's actually in the model's context window, not just to how the instruction itself is phrased.",
            sec_30="Every token in context is re-processed on every turn of an agent loop and is billed accordingly (with output tokens typically costing more per token than input tokens due to autoregressive generation), so bloated, unfocused context directly inflates both cost and latency — and, counter to intuition, can also reduce output quality by distracting the model from what actually matters, making narrow, well-targeted retrieval a genuine correctness lever, not just a cost optimization.",
            min_2="Trace the full pipeline: business requirement -> specification/acceptance contract -> targeted retrieval -> tokenization -> transformer inference -> tool call -> real external execution -> result fed back into context for the next inference step — then give the concrete failure mode (an over-broad context making a simple task slow, expensive, and off-target) and its fix (narrower retrieval, or a fresh subagent with a self-contained brief).",
            deep_technical="Explain why token count doesn't map linearly to word/character count (subword tokenization, with rare words/code/non-English text splitting into more tokens), and why output tokens generally cost more than input tokens (one full autoregressive forward pass per output token, versus largely parallelizable input processing).",
            system_design="Frame multi-agent context isolation (forks vs. fresh agents, per this project's own architecture) as a direct System Design decision about information flow and cost boundaries within an AI-assisted development system.",
            business_value="State plainly that this is a directly measurable cost and quality lever — poor context engineering doubly costs a business (more spent, worse output), unlike most engineering trade-offs where cost and quality pull in opposite directions.",
        ),
        evidence=["agent/rag_index.py", "agent/acceptance_contract.py", "docs/PROJECT_STATE.json"],
        current_status_2026="Context windows at the frontier (1M+ tokens in some current models) have grown dramatically since 2020-2022, but industry consensus by 2025-2026 is that a larger context window does not eliminate the need for deliberate context engineering — 'lost in the middle' and attention-dilution effects (a model attending less reliably to content buried deep within a very large context) remain active, real phenomena, meaning scoped, well-curated context continues to outperform simply including everything available, even when everything technically fits.",
    )


def topic_ai_agent_tool_calling_loop():
    return itopic(
        "AI Agent Tool-Calling Loops & Coding Agents", ESSENTIAL, CURRENT,
        short_overview="How a model goes from 'deciding' to actually changing a real file or running a real command, and why that loop's structure determines an agent's reliability, cost, and blast radius.",
        why="An LLM by itself only predicts text — everything an AI coding agent actually *does* (read a file, run a test, edit code, deploy) happens through a tool-calling loop that hands real execution to real external programs and feeds the real result back in; understanding this loop precisely (not treating the agent as a mysterious autonomous black box) is what lets an engineer design safe autonomy boundaries, debug an agent's mistakes, and reason about its cost/reliability. This project's own subagent/Skills architecture, acceptance contracts, and risk-policy gating are direct, current project experience of exactly this discipline.",
        history_evolution="Early LLM applications (2020-2022) were single-turn: one prompt, one text response, no ability to take real action. Function/tool calling (introduced across major model APIs starting 2023) let a model's output be structured as a request to invoke a named function with specific arguments, which the calling application (never the model itself) would actually execute — this single capability is what turned LLMs from text generators into the execution engine of autonomous agents. ReAct (Yao et al., 2022 paper, 'Reasoning and Acting') formalized the now-standard agent loop pattern of interleaving reasoning ('thought') with action ('tool call') and observation (the tool's real result), repeated until the task is done. 2024-2026 saw this mature into full agentic coding tools (Claude Code and similar) with real filesystem/shell access, subagent delegation, and increasingly fine-grained autonomy/permission models.",
        why_now="Needed whenever an AI system must do more than produce text — any task requiring reading real project state, taking a multi-step action, or verifying a real-world effect. Unnecessary (and needlessly risky) for a genuinely single-shot text-generation or classification task with no real side effects to take.",
        big_picture="Business requirement: an AI agent can autonomously accomplish real engineering tasks (implement a fix, run tests, deploy) while remaining safe, auditable, and correctly scoped — never taking an irreversible or high-risk action without appropriate human oversight. Architecture: a loop of (reason -> select a tool call -> execute it via a real external process -> observe its real result -> reason again) bounded by explicit risk policy (this project's own agent/risk_policy.py deciding what may auto-execute versus what requires human approval) and terminated by a verifiable, pre-defined success condition (an acceptance contract), not by the model's own unverified claim of completion.",
        design_intellect="The core idea is a strict separation between the model (which only reasons and proposes actions in text form) and the tool-execution layer (a real, deterministic external program that actually performs the action and returns a real, truthful result) — this separation is what makes the whole system auditable and controllable: every actual side effect passes through a single, inspectable, policy-gateable chokepoint, rather than the model having any direct, ungoverned access to real systems. The invariant this protects is that autonomy is always bounded by explicit, deterministic policy (risk/complexity-based auto-execution gates) rather than by the model's own self-assessed confidence, which is exactly why this project's own risk_policy.py is structurally separate code from the reasoning it gates.",
        how_exactly="A task/requirement enters the loop -> the model reasons (in its own generated text, sometimes shown as an explicit 'thinking' step) about what to do next -> it emits a structured tool call (a specific function name plus arguments, e.g. 'Read file X' or 'run pytest') -> the surrounding harness (not the model) validates this call against permission/risk policy -> if permitted, the harness actually executes it via a real process (a file read, a real subprocess running a shell command) -> the real result (file contents, command stdout/stderr/exit code) is appended to the conversation as an observation -> the model reasons again, now with this new real information, and either takes another action or, once it believes the task is done, produces a final response -> critically, 'the model believes it's done' is not itself sufficient — a separate, independent verification step (this project's own qa-evaluator, or deterministic tests) checks the claimed result against the original acceptance contract using real evidence, before the task is reported complete.",
        low_level_internals="A tool call is represented in the model's output as a specific structured format (the exact format varies by model/API but is typically JSON-like: a tool name and a set of typed arguments) that the model itself never executes directly — it's parsed by the harness/SDK, dispatched to the actual matching function/process, and the raw result is serialized back into the conversation as the next turn's input, consuming additional context-window tokens on every single tool round trip. A subagent (a nested, separate agent invocation, this project's own Skills/subagent pattern) runs its own independent instance of this exact same loop, with its own separate context window, and returns only a final summary to its parent — deliberately preventing the parent's context from being polluted by the subagent's intermediate tool-call noise.",
        who="The requester (human or upstream process, defining the task/acceptance contract), the model (reasoning and proposing tool calls, never directly executing anything), the harness/tool-execution layer (a real, deterministic program actually performing each action and enforcing permission/risk policy), the real external systems being acted on (the filesystem, a shell, a database, a deployment platform), and an independent verifier (checking the final claimed result against real evidence rather than the agent's own self-report).",
        industry="Tool-calling/agent-loop architecture is now foundational, assumed knowledge across the AI engineering industry in 2025-2026 — 'how would you design the permission model for an autonomous coding agent' is an increasingly common systems-design-style interview question specifically for AI-engineering-adjacent roles.",
        business="An agent loop with weak or missing risk gating can take a high-blast-radius action (a destructive file operation, a production deployment, an irreversible external API call) autonomously and incorrectly — the entire economic case for AI-assisted engineering depends on this specific risk being well-managed, since a single serious autonomous mistake can outweigh a large amount of accumulated productivity gain.",
        product="For a customer-facing AI agent product feature, the exact same loop structure and safety boundary apply — users trust an AI product specifically to the degree its real-world actions are provably bounded and auditable, not merely to the degree its text responses sound confident.",
        cost_economics="Every tool-call round trip costs additional tokens (the tool result is appended to context and re-processed on the next turn) and additional wall-clock latency (a real process must actually execute, e.g. running a real test suite takes real time) — an agent loop that takes many unnecessary or redundant tool calls to accomplish a task is directly more expensive and slower than one that plans efficiently, making 'tool-call efficiency' a genuine, measurable AI-engineering-quality metric (this project's own explicit interest per Section 15).",
        failure_incident="Symptom: an autonomous agent claims a task is complete, but independent verification finds the actual requirement wasn't met (e.g. a claimed bug fix that doesn't actually fix the bug, or a claimed deployment that never took real effect). Telemetry: the agent's own final summary is confident and detailed; a separate, independent check (re-running the original failing scenario, or an independent evaluator using only read-only tools) finds it's still broken. Hypothesis: the agent's self-assessment of success was based on an incomplete or misinterpreted observation (e.g. a deploy tool's status output, not the actual live behavior) rather than genuine end-to-end verification. Diagnosis: comparing what the agent actually verified (which tool calls it made and what results it observed) against what the acceptance contract actually required reveals the gap. Root cause: the loop terminated on the model's own belief of completion rather than on an explicit, independent, evidence-based check. Fix: require every task to terminate against a concrete, pre-defined acceptance contract checked by evidence (real test runs, real production verification), never by the agent's own narrative claim alone — exactly this project's own established qa-evaluator practice. Regression: this becomes a standing process rule (already documented in this project's own CLAUDE.md/lessons) applied to every future task, not a one-off fix. Business impact: without this discipline, an organization can accumulate a false sense of AI-driven progress while real defects silently persist or accumulate, which is a direct trust and quality risk to any business relying on AI-assisted delivery.",
        alternatives_tradeoffs="Fully autonomous execution with no human checkpoint (fastest, highest risk, appropriate only for very low-risk, easily-reversible, well-verified action classes) vs. mandatory human approval before every single tool call (safest, but defeats most of the speed/scale benefit of using an agent at all, appropriate only for the highest-risk actions) vs. risk-based progressive autonomy (this project's own approach: low-risk, reversible, verifiable actions auto-execute; high-risk, irreversible, or ambiguous actions require explicit human approval — the generally-correct middle ground for real production use) vs. self-verification only (the agent checks its own work — faster, but shares the same blind spots as the work itself, which is exactly why independent verification exists as a distinct, additional step).",
        scenario=None,
        interview_mode=im(
            sec_15="A tool-calling loop separates the model (which only reasons and proposes an action in text) from a real execution layer (which actually performs the action and returns a truthful result) — the model reasons, calls a tool, observes the real result, and reasons again, repeated until the task is done and independently verified.",
            sec_30="Every real side effect an AI agent produces passes through one auditable, policy-gateable chokepoint — the tool-execution harness — never through the model directly, which is what makes autonomous agent behavior controllable and safe. Critically, the loop should never terminate on the model's own belief that it's finished; it should terminate against a concrete, pre-defined, evidence-checked acceptance contract, since a model's self-assessment shares the same blind spots as its own work.",
            min_2="Walk the full loop (reason, tool call, real execution, observation, reason again) with a concrete example, explain why risk-based auto-execution policy must be separate deterministic code from the model's reasoning, then use the 'agent claims done, independent check finds it isn't' incident to show why self-verification alone is insufficient.",
            deep_technical="Explain why every tool-call round trip re-processes and re-bills the growing context (each result gets appended and resent), and why this makes tool-call efficiency a genuine, measurable performance/cost metric for an agent's design, not just its underlying model.",
            system_design="Frame the permission/risk-policy layer as a first-class System Design component of any autonomous agent architecture — a specific, answerable design question ('what may this agent do without human approval, and why') rather than an afterthought.",
            business_value="State directly that the ROI case for AI-assisted engineering depends on managing autonomous-mistake risk well — a small number of serious ungated mistakes can outweigh a large amount of accumulated productivity gain, making the safety architecture itself a business-value lever, not just a technical concern.",
            incident_debugging="Use the specific diagnostic move: compare exactly which tool calls were made and what real results were observed against what the acceptance contract actually required, rather than trusting the agent's own narrative summary.",
        ),
        evidence=["agent/risk_policy.py", "agent/acceptance_contract.py", ".claude/agents/qa-evaluator.md"],
        current_status_2026="Standardized protocols for exposing tools to models (notably MCP, the Model Context Protocol, introduced late 2024 and widely adopted through 2025-2026) have made tool integration increasingly interoperable across different agent frameworks and models, rather than every tool needing bespoke per-framework integration — a significant maturation of the tool-calling ecosystem since the earlier, more fragmented 2023-era function-calling implementations.",
    )


